<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<%- include('partials/head', { title }) %>
<body>
    <header>
        <p class="site-name">Operating Systems</p>
        <%- include('partials/nav', { currentPage }) %>
    </header>

    <main>
        <h1>Operating-System Families</h1>

        <p>Operating systems are designed for different devices and needs. Here are several common categories.</p>

        <section class="family-grid" aria-label="Operating-system categories">
            <article class="family-card">
                <img class="family-image" src="/img/os-desktop.svg" alt="Desktop computer">
                <h2>Desktop and laptop systems</h2>
                <p>These systems support general-purpose computing, including browsing, creative work, and software development. Examples include Windows, macOS, and Linux.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="family-card">
                <img class="family-image" src="/img/os-mobile.svg" alt="Smartphone">
                <h2>Mobile systems</h2>
                <p>Mobile operating systems are designed for phones and tablets. They focus on touch input, battery life, wireless connections, and apps. Examples include Android and iOS.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="family-card">
                <img class="family-image" src="/img/os-server.svg" alt="Server rack">
                <h2>Server systems</h2>
                <p>Server operating systems provide services such as websites, file storage, and databases. They often run without a desktop interface. Examples include Linux and Windows Server.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="family-card">
                <img class="family-image" src="/img/os-embedded.svg" alt="Embedded device controller">
                <h2>Embedded and real-time systems</h2>
                <p>Embedded operating systems run inside dedicated devices such as appliances and vehicles. Some real-time systems are designed to respond to events within strict time limits.</p>
            </article>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Quick comparison</h2>
            <table>
                <tr><th>Category</th><th>Common priority</th><th>Typical interaction</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Desktop</td><td>Versatility and productivity</td><td>Keyboard, mouse, and display</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Mobile</td><td>Touch, battery, and connectivity</td><td>Touchscreen and sensors</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Server</td><td>Reliability and serving requests</td><td>Remote administration</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Embedded</td><td>Dedicated tasks and efficiency</td><td>Often built into another device</td></tr>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section class="book-source">
            <h2>Book source</h2>
            <p>For further reading: <a href="https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/">Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces</a> by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and Andrea C. Arpaci-Dusseau.</p>
        </section>
        <p><a href="/os-history">Next: explore a brief history</a></p>
    </main>

    <%- include('partials/footer') %>
</body>
</html>
